summary:
@eshear concrete examples:
- if you're coordinating people to do something together, being confident that people will all agree to come along causes you to project the kind of confidence that makes people actually come along with you
- there are many things where you need to either commit to the bit or not embark at all. if you're not confident you can commit to the bit, you will rationally not embark, because you know you might bail. but it's also a stable (and rational) equilibrium to be confident you'll commit to the bit.
